      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While structural forces like globalization and technology create powerful headwinds, they are not strictly \"inevitable,\" as history shows well-designed policies can decouple innovation from extreme inequality.\n\nProponents argue that strategic government intervention in education, restructuring of the tax code, and regulation of financial markets can mitigate these structural pressures without stalling economic growth.\n\nHowever, critics contend that without such deliberate political will, the entrenched incentives of the current market system naturally favor capital over labor, making the gap difficult to reverse.\n\nUltimately, the debate centers on whether political choices can successfully counterbalance these deep-seated economic trends or if the system is indeed self-reinforcing.",
